Phase 2 — The Living History Engine

Instead of just looking at two perspectives, imagine being able to enter any person’s historical position, move through time, and watch their understanding of the world change.

The next five capabilities

* Knowledge Horizon Simulator — Reconstruct what an individual could know at a given moment, what they couldn’t, and how information reached them.
* Memory Genealogy — Trace how an event became eyewitness testimony, family memory, mythology, education, and eventually accepted history.
* Perspective Switching — Move between individual, class, institution, empire, and civilization without losing the underlying historical event.
* Historical Delta Engine — Detect contradictions, missing witnesses, information asymmetries, and meaningful changes in interpretation.
* Cross-Species Chronoscope — Compare human cultural inheritance with animal traditions, evolutionary inheritance, and ecological records while respecting their different mechanisms.

The first real experiment

I’d use Iran in 1953 as our proving ground because it fits the conversation that led us here.

Imagine selecting August 1953 and switching between:

* An Iranian university student
* A rural Iranian farmer
* A British intelligence officer
* An American diplomat
* A supporter of the Shah
* A historian researching the events in 2026

Then move forward ten years, twenty years, fifty years.

Watch what each perspective gains, loses, changes, or passes to another generation.

The crucial new feature would be information flow: who knew what, when, through which channel, and with what reliability.

